.TH TEXRS 1 "2026" "texrs 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
texrs \- a TeX engine: Knuth's mouth and expander, lowered onto a bytecode VM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B texrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR[.tex]]... [\fICOMMANDS\fR]
.br
.B texrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\e\fIFIRST-LINE\fR
.br
.B texrs
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB&\fIFMT\fR \fIARGS\fR
.br
.B texrs
.SH DESCRIPTION
Invoked the way
.BR tex (1)
is invoked.  A file name without an extension gets
.BR .tex ;
arguments after the file are more TeX input, read after it, unless the file's
own
.B \eend
stopped the run first; a first argument beginning with
.B \e
makes the whole argument list a line of input with no file at all; and
.B texrs
with no arguments opens the prompt, because an engine given nothing to do should
ask rather than print its usage and quit.
.PP
.B texrs
reads a TeX file, tokenises it under a mutable category\-code table, expands
the macros, and lowers what is left to
.B fusevm
bytecode which the shared VM executes.  Nothing in texrs interprets TeX: the
program runs as bytecode, the same way
.BR zshrs ,
.BR stryke ,
.BR rubylang ,
and
.B pythonrs
run on that engine.
.PP
TeX is two machines.  The
.I mouth
turns bytes into tokens; the
.I expander
turns tokens into other tokens \(em
.BR \edef ,
.BR \ecsname ,
.BR \ethe ,
the conditionals.  Only after that does the
.I stomach
build boxes and ship DVI.  texrs implements the first two, which is the half a
macro\-heavy document spends its time in.  It is
.B not
a typesetter: there are no boxes, no glue, no paragraph breaking, no fonts and
no DVI, and
.B \eend
stops the run rather than shipping a page.
.PP
What is carried: category codes and
.BR \ecatcode ,
the three\-state line scanner,
.B ^^X
notation,
.B \edef
with undelimited and delimited parameters,
.BR \elet ,
.BR \eedef / \exdef ,
.BR \egdef ,
.BR \eglobal ,
.BR \ebegingroup / \eendgroup
and brace groups,
.BR \ecsname / \eendcsname ,
.BR \estring ,
.BR \ethe ,
.BR \enumber ,
.BR \eexpandafter ,
.BR \enoexpand ,
the conditionals
.RB ( \eiftrue ,
.BR \eiffalse ,
.BR \eifnum ,
.BR \eifodd ,
.BR \eifx ,
.BR \eifcase
with
.BR \eor ,
.BR \eelse ,
.BR \efi ),
the 256
.B \ecount
registers with
.BR \eadvance / \emultiply / \edivide ,
and
.BR \emessage .
For what is not carried, see
.B BUGS.md
in the source tree.
.SH DOCUMENTS
A document larger than one file is described by a
.I Texrs.toml
at its root, and the
.B \-X
commands act on that document rather than on a file \(em the shape
.BR tectonic (1)
gives its own V2 interface.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[doc]
name = "thesis"
inputs = ["macros.tex", "body.tex"]

[[output]]
name = "default"
type = "messages"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A
.B bundle
key names a zip of support files, searched after the document's own directory
and its extra paths: what a document carries with it when it is shared as one
file rather than as a directory somebody has to keep in order.  A file beside
the document overrides the one in its bundle, so a recipient can change a macro
without unpacking the archive.  Unlike
.BR tectonic (1),
whose bundle is fetched, this one is a path \(em a build that reaches the
network is one that fails on an aeroplane and depends on a server still
answering in ten years.  Fetch it with whatever fetches things and hand the file
over.
.PP
A bundle may also be named by the digest of its bytes,
.BR sha256: \fIDIGEST\fR ,
which resolves to one that
.B \-X bundle fetch
has put in the cache.  A URL in that key is refused with the command that
fetches it: a build never reaches the network, so what a document names has to
be bytes that are already here.  Naming the digest rather than the address is
also what makes a build reproducible \(em an address serves whatever it serves
today, a digest is the file the document was written against.
.TP
.B \-X bundle fetch " \fIURL\fR"
Download an archive over http or https and file it under the digest of its
bytes, printing the
.B sha256:
name to put in
.IR Texrs.toml .
This is the only thing in texrs that uses the network, and it does so only when
asked.  A response larger than a hundred megabytes is refused rather than
written: a bundle is macro files, and anything that size is a mistake or a
hostile server.
.TP
.BI \-X " dvi \fIFILE\fR.dvi"
Read what real
.BR tex (1)
shipped for a document and print what is in it: the preamble's comment, each
page with its
.BR \ecount0 ,
the fonts it defines, its specials, and the characters it sets.
.PP
texrs writes no DVI \(em it stops where the boxes would begin \(em so this
reads one rather than writing it. The parity contract stops in the same place:
today it compares the
.B \emessage
stream, and the moment texrs sets a character the reference to compare against
is what tex shipped. This is the reading half of that comparison, written while
the format can still be checked against a document whose output is known.
XeTeX's extensions (opcodes 252\(en254) are named rather than decoded: pointing
this at an
.I .xdv
should say what the file is rather than fail as if it were damaged.
.TP
.B \-X bundle list
Print the bundles in the cache with the file each is stored in.
.PP
A
.B preamble
key names files read before the inputs and kept as a FORMAT: a macro file is
compiled once and every later build starts from what it left, which is what
.BR tex (1)
does with
.B \edump
and why LaTeX starts in milliseconds rather than seconds.  A preamble may only
DEFINE \(em category codes, macros,
.B \elet
\(em because a format holds state and has nowhere to keep work.  One that does
something (a
.BR \emessage ,
a
.B \ecount
assignment) is not made into a format and the document is compiled whole
instead, since dropping what it did would change what the document means.
.PP
The inputs are read in order and compiled as one document, and what each of them
hashed to is recorded, so a build says exactly what it read. An output
.B type
is a pipeline this engine has:
.BR messages ,
.B tokens
or
.BR disasm .
There is no
.BR bundle ,
.BR tex_format ,
.B shell_escape
or
.B synctex
key, as there is in tectonic's schema: texrs has no package universe, one
format, no shell and no page, and a key describing machinery that does not exist
would be a promise the engine cannot keep.
.TP
.B \-X new " [" \fIDIR\fR ]
Write a
.I Texrs.toml
and an
.I index.tex
into
.I DIR
(the current directory by default), named after it. The starting document sets
the category codes INITEX leaves ordinary, because one that did not would fail
on its first group. An existing document is never overwritten.
.TP
.B \-X build " [" \-\-profile " \fINAME\fR ]"
Build the document this directory is in, found by walking up, into its
.I build/
directory. With one output the profile need not be named; with several, the one
called
.B default
is used unless
.B \-\-profile
says otherwise.
.TP
.B \-X init
Write a document into the current directory, named after it \(em
.B \-X new .
under the name tectonic gives it, because "make one here" is what a user reaches
for in a directory that already exists.
.TP
.B \-X show
Print what the document is: where its source and build directories are, the
inputs it is made of with the first twelve characters of each one's digest, and
the outputs it can produce with the file each would write. Everything is read
from the document and its inputs, so it says what a build would use rather than
what one used last.
.TP
.B \-X dump " [" \-\-profile " \fINAME\fR ]"
Build a profile to standard output, writing nothing. What it prints is what
.B \-X build
would have written, so a pipeline can read a build without hunting for the file
or leaving one behind.
.TP
.B \-X watch " [" \-\-profile " \fINAME\fR ] [" \-\-interval " \fIMS\fR ]"
Build the document, then build it again whenever an input changes, until
interrupted. Change is decided by the digest of each input rather than by its
mtime, so a file touched but not edited does not rebuild, and neither does an
editor that saves through a temporary file \(em which most do, and which moves
the mtime while the content stands still. A document that does not compile is
reported and the watch carries on: that is the normal state of one being
edited.
.PP
A command that is not one of these is looked for on
.B PATH
as
.BI texrs- name
and run with the remaining arguments, which is how tectonic lets a command be
added without changing the binary:
.B texrs \-X lint
runs
.BR texrs-lint .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS tex's options
Accepted in
.BR tex 's
own spelling, with one dash or two and a value after either an
.B =
or a space, so an invocation written for
.B tex
drives texrs unchanged.  This milestone acts on
.BR \-interaction ;
the rest are recorded and reported by
.B \-X show
but change nothing yet, because what they configure \(em the log file, the
format, the DVI \(em is the stomach texrs does not have.
.TP
.BI \-interaction= MODE
.BR batchmode ", " nonstopmode ", " scrollmode " or " errorstopmode .
.B batchmode
writes nothing to the terminal; texrs never stops to ask, so the other three
differ only in what they would do at an error.
.TP
.BI \-jobname= NAME
Set the job name.
.TP
.BI \-output\-directory= DIR
Write files in
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI \-progname= NAME
Set the program name.
.TP
.BI \-fmt= NAME
Use a named format, as
.BI & NAME
does.
.TP
.B \-ini
Be initex.  texrs loads no format either way, so it already is.
.TP
.B \-halt\-on\-error
Stop at the first error, which texrs does regardless.
.TP
.BR \-file\-line\-error ", " \-no\-file\-line\-error
Enable or disable
.B file:line:error
style messages.
.TP
.B \-recorder
Record the files read.
.TP
.B \-8bit
Write 8\-bit characters as themselves rather than as
.BR ^^ -escapes.
.SS texrs's options
.TP
.B \-\-repl
Start the interactive prompt.  A line is a line of TeX read with every line
before it still in effect: a
.B \ecatcode
set on one prompt changes how the next one READS, a
.B \edef
changes what it means, and a register assignment survives \(em the session
re-runs the document it has built rather than evaluating each line in a vacuum.
A line that fails is rolled back rather than left in the document, and
.B \eend
ends the line, not the session.  Tab completes the primitives; history lives in
.IR ~/.texrs/history .
With stdin on a pipe the line editor is skipped and lines are read plainly, so
.B texrs \-\-repl < doc.tex
works.
.TP
.B \-\-lsp
Speak the Language Server Protocol over stdio.  Completion and hover come from
the same primitive corpus that generates the reference documentation, and
diagnostics come from the engine's own lowerer \(em a document the editor shows
as clean is a document
.B texrs
will run.  Nothing is written to the terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-dap
Speak the Debug Adapter Protocol over stdio: source\-line breakpoints, single
stepping, a stack frame, and the
.B \ecount
registers as the variables scope.  What is debuggable is what survives
lowering \(em macros expand at COMPILE time, so a breakpoint stops on the lines
that left run\-time work behind, and one set on a line that did not is reported
unverified rather than silently never firing.  The debug run is interpreted:
the tracing JIT would compile the markers away.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-tokens
Print the mouth's token stream for the file and exit.  No expansion happens,
so what prints is what the category codes made of the bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print the lowered fusevm bytecode disassembly and exit.  A
.B \ecount
register is a VM slot, so
.B \eadvance\ecount0 by 5
reads as
.BR "GetSlot / LoadInt / Add / SetSlot" .
.TP
.B \-\-aot
Compile the document to a standalone native executable beside it \(em
.I doc.tex
becomes
.IR doc ,
the way a C file becomes a program.  The chunk is emitted as a relocatable
object through fusevm's ahead\-of\-time compiler and linked against the texrs
runtime, so the result runs with no interpreter dispatch loop and needs no texrs
on the machine.  It prints exactly what an ordinary run prints.
.IP
Requires a C toolchain for the link step.
.B TEXRS_STATICLIB
names the runtime library to link against when it is not beside the binary.
.TP
.B \-\-tiers
Run the document, then report what fusevm's tiers did with its bytecode: how
many ops the chunk holds, whether the block tier accepts and has compiled it,
the largest block\-eligible op range, every loop header and whether the tracing
tier compiled it, and the ops the block tier refuses with their counts.  The
answers come from fusevm's own predicates, not from an assumption about them \(em
enabling the JIT is not the same as being compiled by it.
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs= N
Compile
.I N
documents at once when several files are named, one thread each; the default is
one per core. Output is printed in ARGUMENT order however the threads finish,
because a build log that reorders itself between runs is not one anyone can
diff, and the exit status is the worst of the runs. A single document is
compiled by one thread either way \(em this parallelises the batch, not the
engine.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cache
Compile this run rather than reading the bytecode cache.  The result is the
same either way; this is how a run is timed against a cold one, or how a cache
that is suspected of being wrong is ruled out.
.TP
.B \-\-cache\-stats
Print where the cache is, how many documents it holds and how large it is, then
exit.  With
.B TEXRS_CACHE
set to
.BR 0 ,
.B false
or
.BR no ,
it says the cache is off and names the path it would have used.
.TP
.B \-\-cache\-clear
Delete the cache and exit.  Every document compiles again on its next run.
Deleting a cache is never destructive: it holds only what can be recompiled.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option grammar and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version banner and exit.
.SH INTERCEPTS
.B \eintercept{before|after|around}{<glob>}{\ehandler}
registers advice on macro expansion:
.RS
.nf
\edef\egreet#1{HELLO-#1}
\edef\etrace{[in]}
\eintercept{before}{greet}{\etrace}
\emessage{\egreet{WORLD}}      % => [in]HELLO-WORLD
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B before
puts the handler's body in front of the expansion,
.B after
puts it behind, and
.B around
replaces it, with
.B \eproceed
standing for what the macro would have expanded to \(em a handler with no
.B \eproceed
suppresses the call.
.PP
The pattern is a GLOB over macro names, which is what makes advice useful on a
macro package: it is registered before the macros it will catch exist.  The
handler is a macro that takes no parameters; one that takes them is refused
rather than called with whatever followed the intercepted call.
.PP
Expansion is a compile\-time act, so advice is woven into the token stream and
is undone by the group that registered it, like any other assignment.  A handler
that calls the macro it advises does not weave itself: a call inside advice is
not advised.
.SH INLINE RUST
A
.B \erust{ \&... }
block is compiled by
.BR rustc ,
loaded, and its
.B #[no_mangle] pub extern "C"
functions become callable from the document:
.RS
.nf
\erust{
    #[no_mangle]
    pub extern "C" fn twice(n: i64) -> i64 { n * 2 }
}
\ecatcode`\e{=1 \ecatcode`\e}=2
\ecount1=21
\emessage{\erustcall twice \ecount1 \eendrust}   % => 42
.fi
.RE
.PP
The block body is Rust, not TeX, so it is lifted out of the file BEFORE the
mouth reads it \(em
.BR # ,
.BR { ,
.B }
and
.B &
are category codes the mouth would act on.  A call is a NUMBER wherever TeX
reads one: a register assignment, an arithmetic operand, a conditional, or a
message body.  In running text it is called for its effect and the value is
dropped.
.PP
The compiled library is cached by body hash, so only the first run pays for the
compile.  A block that does not compile stops the run with rustc's own
diagnostic;
.B RUSTC
names the compiler to use.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
The file could not be read, an option was not recognised, or the engine
stopped.  Engine errors are written to stderr as
.BR "! <reason>." ,
the way tex writes them.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Run a file and see its
.B \emessage
stream, printed the way tex prints it:
.RS
.nf
texrs hello.tex
(./hello.tex HELLO\-WORLD )
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read the bytecode a document lowered to:
.RS
.nf
texrs \-\-disasm examples/counters.tex
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read the token stream the mouth produced:
.RS
.nf
texrs \-\-dump\-tokens examples/macros.tex
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/texrs/scripts.rkyv
The bytecode cache: an rkyv shard of source path to compiled
.B fusevm::Chunk
, written after a document compiles and read instead of compiling it again.
Entries carry the source's mtime and the mtime of the texrs that wrote them, so
an edited document or a rebuilt texrs is a miss rather than a stale hit, and a
shard this build cannot read is rebuilt rather than misread.  On macOS the path
is under
.IR ~/Library/Caches .
The shard's header carries the magic
.B TEXR
and follows the header convention the sibling engines share, so
.BR zdbview (1)
opens it as a key/value cache.
.TP
.I examples/*.tex
Runnable examples, each held in parity with real tex by the test suite.
.TP
.I tests/cases/*.tex
The differential corpus.
.TP
.I tests/known_gaps.txt
Every case that does not match tex yet, with the reason.
